WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.280
One of the most amazing things about&nbsp;
the parallel growth of data science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.840
and genetics has been the growth&nbsp;
of data. The human genome has about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.760
40,000 genes and that in turn is made&nbsp;
up of about three million base pairs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.360
The first human genome was sequenced in&nbsp;
2001 and took several years to complete.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.440
But in recent years, researchers have sequenced&nbsp;
thousands of genome and it’s becoming relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.640
simple task for a lab to do this nowadays.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.760
What this means is that, genetic data is now&nbsp;
produced much faster than it can be organized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.760
or analyzed or interpreted or implemented. Part&nbsp;
of this is that the methods for structuring data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.160
that is, having a shared way of putting it&nbsp;
together and making it easy to transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.880
between labs and between researchers, as well&nbsp;
as methods of communicating the findings lag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.480
dramatically behind the development of the ability&nbsp;
to get data. Data’s a good thing, but you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.840
be able to make sense of it. Indeed, we’ve a&nbsp;
lot of data that is potentially of great use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.000
There’s a lot of untapped potential. Let’s&nbsp;
discuss about a few of the things that had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.080
particularly successful in using genetics&nbsp;
and the data science of healthcare.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.920
Number one is predicting disease risks&nbsp;
through genetics. When most people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.480
think of genetics and disease, they think of&nbsp;
single gene causes like the gene for cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:35.520
But things are rarely so simple. Most diseases&nbsp;
involve what are called polygenic interactions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.840
or the combined effect of many genes,&nbsp;
possibly thousands all at once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.960
The enormous increase in the amount of data&nbsp;
available, along with the development of data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.880
science have made it possible to detect many of&nbsp;
these polygenic effects. So, they can actually now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.720
say, this combination, for instance, of 400 genes&nbsp;
collectively work to predict a particular disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.640
There’s a unique study on Parkinson’s disease,&nbsp;
conducted as part of the Parkinson’s progression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.440
markers initiative, that made it possible for&nbsp;
researchers to combine data from genetics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.000
from imaging, from clinical data, and from&nbsp;
demographics data to altogether develop a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.000
highly accurate model for predicting a&nbsp;
patient’s risk of Parkinson’s disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.720
Similarly, researchers at other institutions have&nbsp;
also made progress on mining DNA data to predict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.160
diseases ranging from coronary artery disease to&nbsp;
breast cancer to diabetes and a host of others.&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.280
But it’s important to remember that DNA is not&nbsp;
destiny. Even in identical twins who share the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:43.360
same DNA, diseases do not show up the same.&nbsp;
Really, what data science do is they give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.720
probabilities but not a concrete this is the&nbsp;
way it is. And that’s important to remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.280
because not only are there test for things&nbsp;
like certain kinds of cancer or Parkinson’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.520
but also researchers have been developing&nbsp;
test for things like IQ and mental illness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.360
Some of which, because they’re based on DNA, can&nbsp;
be administered while the embryo is still in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.920
womb. And this raises a huge number of ethical&nbsp;
dilemmas. We’ll talk about some of these later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.480
but it recalls the movie Gattaca where people&nbsp;
were categorized and really channeled according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:20.000
to their genetic potential. It’s important to&nbsp;
remember that in something like identical twins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:27.040
who again, have 100% the same DNA, things like&nbsp;
schizophrenia, major mental illness, only 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:33.280
concordance. What that means is if one twin&nbsp;
has developed schizophrenia, there’s only a 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:39.200
chance, a flip of a coin, that the other one has&nbsp;
it as well. So, it’s important to be a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.240
humble, a little circumspect when interpreting the&nbsp;
probabilities that come from predictive analytics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:50.720
for diseases through DNA. And there’s also, a&nbsp;
need to focus on controllable factors, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.760
when you look at things like heart disease.&nbsp;
There’s so many things that a person can do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:01.440
like diet and exercise, that can help reduce the&nbsp;
person’s risk, even if it never changes their DNA.&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.000
&nbsp;
But keeping that all in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.560
the ability to gather huge amounts of genetic&nbsp;
data and the mechanisms that are making it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.280
easier to sort through potentially trillions&nbsp;
of data points, to try to find an association&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.120
with the disease are bringing exciting&nbsp;
potential for finding and diagnosing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:27.840
diseases and understanding their nature through&nbsp;
the combination of genetics and data science.

